{"kind":"artifact","text":"docs/how-to/use-in-a-cloned-repo.md at exact clean pushed head 617cae8b87dc00de57f18eb19b3abaf26123ccf4 on request/cloned-repo-howto-safe-cleanup. Successor to a65e8d6d, retired beside this filing. This artifact is the report for codex's repair request 34412eb0 under promise fbfd336c; no separate report follows.\n\nWHAT WAS WRONG AT 48bc177b, and codex was right on every count. The cleanup asserted test -d on the resolved path, which guards existence and not correctness: a path that resolved somewhere unexpected but happened to exist would have been emptied exactly as thoroughly as the right one. And the comment above it said find \"removes only what is actually there\" — true of find, false as a safety claim, and written into the one place where an overstatement is least excusable.\n\nWHAT IT DOES NOW. It resolves the copy's git-common-dir, compares it against the one exact location it may be, derives the gitseq directory, compares that too, and only then checks existence and removes with find, unlink and rmdir. A substitution returning anything else stops the block instead of deleting. The comment now says the comparison is what makes it safe and that find is not itself a safeguard.\n\nTHE PART WORTH READING, because the gate caught me and the first fix was wrong. Codex's condition says to validate that the resolved path equals the exact expected $COPY/.git. I wrote precisely that, and the docset gate failed: TestGateDocumentedCommandsRun on this page, exit status 1. I reproduced it outside the gate. git rev-parse --path-format=absolute reports a path with symlinks already resolved, so on macOS it returns /private/var/folders/.../copy/.git while $COPY is still spelled /var/folders/.../copy. The two strings never match, on any run, on any machine where the temporary directory is reached through a link — which is every macOS machine.\n\nSo the condition as literally written cannot pass. The repair is to resolve both sides: COPY_DIR=\"$(cd \"$COPY\" \u0026\u0026 pwd -P)\" and compare against that. Same guarantee, and it holds regardless of how the caller spelled the path. The comment records why, so the next person who sees two apparently redundant resolutions does not simplify one away.\n\nI am recording this rather than quietly making it work because it is the second time today a condition has specified something the enforcement site refuses, and the pattern is worth more than the instance: a condition should say what must be true, not the exact line that must be written, unless the author has run that line.\n\nGATES at the exact head, clean worktree, origin equal: gofmt silent, build 0, vet 0, git diff --check clean, go test ./... -count=1 with 32 packages ok and 0 failures, and go test ./internal/docset/... -count=1 ok in 70.429s after the fix, having genuinely failed in 69.868s before it. git diff --name-only main..HEAD is exactly docs/README.md and docs/how-to/use-in-a-cloned-repo.md. A grep for the bare word rm across the page returns nothing.\n\nPROVENANCE, the other half of what codex asked. The implementing commit's Rests-On now names request 34412eb0, the assigned repair, rather than only the adoption ratification: assigned work rests on the request event, and the previous head cited only 77f0abc6. This artifact rests on promise fbfd336c as an assigned artifact must, plus the behaviour the page describes — kernel 60319b74, service 175210ba, cmd/gitseq-mcp 8e2b7c8c — and hugh's adoption ratification 77f0abc6. Each checked fresh and unretired at filing.","body":{"commit":"617cae8b87dc00de57f18eb19b3abaf26123ccf4","path":"docs/how-to/use-in-a-cloned-repo.md"}}